From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: caomengxuan666 <2507560089@qq.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:17:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] perf(ci): drop sccache and cache the release dependencies
 directly

The sccache approach was measured and is a regression, so remove it rather than
tune it. Run 39008835942 (sccache on the local disk cache, 4G) was cancelled at
1h55m still inside "Build the release binaries"; the unwrapped run 38998544353
finished its build in 68 minutes. Neither sccache nor its configuration failed:
the log shows `SCCACHE_BACKEND: disk` and RUSTC_WRAPPER pointing at sccache.exe
with no cache errors. It was simply slower.

Where the time goes, from the unwrapped run's log:

- ~725 dependency crates compiled between 03:24:33 and 03:31, about seven minutes.
- The next ~290 crates started between 03:31 and 03:51.
- From 03:51 until `Finished` at 04:22:22 -- 31 minutes -- no new crate started.

That last window is the first-party crates plus the `lto = "thin"` link, which
change on every commit. sccache has to hash the whole thin-LTO input set and
store the ~300 MB codex-cli output for that step, so its overhead lands squarely
on the longest single unit of work. Hence the regression.

What replaces it: cache the compiled dependency artifacts under
`codex-rs/target/release` (deps, .fingerprint, build) with actions/cache, keyed
on the lockfile, toolchain and rustc flags -- never on the source, so a new
commit still hits it and cargo's fingerprints decide what to rebuild. Save only
after a successful build and only on a miss, because an exact key can never be
overwritten and a partial tree saved under it would be permanent.

This is expected to recover the dependency portion only, not the hour. To stop
guessing about the rest, the build now runs with `--timings` and uploads
cargo-timing.html, matching what the repo's own release workflow does. The next
run's artifact will say how much is compile and how much is link.
